@StormLiangMS The requested exact-head 202605 hardware validation is complete.

On `testbed-bjw2-can-720dt-5` with `SONiC.20260510.12` (`644c1a022f`), the live detector confirmed PCI ID `b274` resolves to `td3`. Pretest passed, and the initial target plus five consecutive runs passed all four affected IPv4 combinations: 24/24 affected cases, zero failures/errors. Exact-head CI and the post-undraft Copilot review are also clean.

The PR is ready for formal review and the 202605 backport decision.

@Ryangwaite Thanks for fixing this. We encountered the same regression on a Mellanox-SN4700-O8V48 testbed: `hwsku.json` supplied the 200G platform default, while the connection graph specified 100G for twelve physical links. `deploy-mg` consequently restored 200G and left those ports oper-down.

The source tracking and precedence change in `minigraph_device.j2` look correct. However,  `ansible/templates/wan_minigraph_device.j2` still uses the old port_speed -> breakout_speed -> device_conn ordering.  `config_wan_sonic_testbed.yml` gathers the same port_alias and connection-graph facts and renders this WAN template, so JSON-backed WAN deployments appear to retain the same regression.

Could you apply the same port_speed_is_platform_default precedence logic to `wan_minigraph_device.j2` , or clarify why that rendering path is unaffected?

@r12f @Pterosaur for viz

@saiarcot895 — good questions:

1. No upstream CI link; the failure is on our internal Chipmunk BMC testbed where the PTF is a standalone `docker run` (no vs/ptf32 topology). CI passes because the KVM topology wires a `mgmt` veth into the PTF, and many CI PTF images still ship classic `ntp`. Both conditions have to be true to hit this.
2. Not a topology change — standalone PTF containers have only had `eth0` all along; the `mgmt` interface is added by the `vs`/KVM topology wiring, not by the PTF image itself. The helper was implicitly relying on that; the probe just makes it work in both cases.

